The conformance check identifies that the render function's stale-node removal loop doesn't have a parentNode guard before calling `removeChild`.

Looking at app.js, in the `render()` function, lines ~175-178:
```javascript
for (var id in existingItems) {
  if (!filteredIds[id]) {
    existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id]);
  } else {
    reuseItems[id] = existingItems[id];
  }
}
```

The conformance check says: "Guard every removeChild call in the stale-node removal loop with a parentNode check." The current code does `existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id])` without first checking if `existingItems[id].parentNode` is truthy.

The fix is to add a parentNode guard:
```javascript
if (existingItems[id].parentNode) {
  existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id]);
}
```

Wait, but I also see the blur handler in app.js at line ~354:
```javascript
on(todoList, 'blur', function (e) {
  var input = e.target.closest('.edit');
  if (!input) return;
  var li = input.closest('li');
  if (!li) return;
  if (!li.classList.contains('editing')) return;
  var id = todoId(li);
  editTodo(id, input.value);
}, true);
```

This blur handler with `capture: true` means when a blur happens during removeChild, it could re-enter render() through editTodo, which would try to remove the same li again. The parentNode guard makes it idempotent.

